From: TrailCurrent Godwit
Subject: [PATCH] rtnl: read whole netlink replies

netlink_event() read rtnetlink messages into a 4096-byte buffer, and a
netlink datagram larger than the buffer is truncated without notice. On a
kernel with 16 KiB pages (Raspberry Pi 5) a link dump reply can carry up
to 8 KiB of links, so every link after the first 4 KiB was lost at
startup: with two CAN interfaces ahead of it, wlan0 was never seen and
Wi-Fi could not scan until it was turned off and on again.

Read into a 32 KiB buffer, the largest reply the kernel sends.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/rtnl.c
+++ b/src/rtnl.c
@@ -1472,7 +1472,14 @@
 
 static gboolean netlink_event(GIOChannel *chan, GIOCondition cond, gpointer data)
 {
-	unsigned char buf[4096];
+	/*
+	 * Netlink replies are datagrams: whatever does not fit the buffer is
+	 * dropped. A link dump packs as many links into one reply as the
+	 * kernel's buffer holds, which is up to 8 KiB on a 16 KiB-page kernel
+	 * (Raspberry Pi 5), so a 4 KiB buffer silently lost every link past the
+	 * first few. 32 KiB is the most the kernel puts in one reply.
+	 */
+	unsigned char buf[32768];
 	struct sockaddr_nl nladdr;
 	socklen_t addr_len = sizeof(nladdr);
 	ssize_t status;
